    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed that sometimes when playing certain notes, there's this subtle, almost \"ghostly\" quality to the sound where you hear these faint, unintended frequencies that don't quite feel like they belong to the main note. Is there a specific mathematical reason why those extra partials can sometimes sound slightly detached or \"hidden\" within the tone?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those subtle, detached frequencies are likely the result of non-linearities present in the air column and the player's embouchure, leading to the excitation of inharmonic partials. Inharmonicity means the overtones do not fall into the perfect integer ratios expected in a pure harmonic series. This slight mismatch between expected and actual frequencies creates beating or interference patterns that our ears perceive as faint, \"ghostly\" side sounds rather than integrated harmonics.",
